{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0131WumrgmpzrQ8mjiwZ8Wdc","type":"tool_result","content":"1:# my-3D2dge guide for AI models\n7:## North star\n17:## The mental model\n29:## Remake workflow (port, remaster, reimagine, remix)\n44:## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\n64:## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\n72:## Hard rules\n82:## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\n120:## Frame order\n140:## Systems in more depth\n142:### Views and resolution\n150:### Scenes\n155:### Camera\n163:### Players (two to four on one machine)\n169:### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\n178:### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n190:### Heights: roofs, ledges, sight and reach\n222:### PlatformMap (side-scrolling levels)\n242:### Platformer (side-scroller controller)\n249:### Body (ground-plane physics)\n255:### Mover and actions (third-person ground controller)\n267:### Perception (sight, hearing, suspicion)\n273:### Attack (melee timing)\n280:### Bullets and patterns\n286:### Humanoid\n297:### Audio\n305:### Text and UI\n311:### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\n319:### Lighting\n328:### Errors and warnings\n338:### Testing without a browser\n357:### The handoff package\n360:### Modules (general systems in files of their own)\n367:### Kits and the diet (which file for which game)\n374:### Recorded sessions (a bug from a phone, replayed exactly)\n402:## Genre recipes\n606:## Building a big game: lessons from Emberdeep\n631:## Where to find more: animation and examples\n648:## Performance\n656:## Checklist before handing a game back\n----\n55:- 219 Views (projections); E.View: view\n223:- `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale = 1, zBoost = 1) → view`: new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost): an orthographic camera (degrees; scale = screen pixels per world unit; zBoost stretches heights)\n221:- `E.VIEWS: iso, threequarter, topdown, brawler, side, overhead`: the ready-made views: iso (yaw 45, pitch 30), threequarter (pitch 55), topdown (80), brawler (25), side (0) and overhead (90, scale 1: world x/y = screen pixels); new E.Game({ view: 'iso' }) and game.setView('side') take their names\n222:- `E.VIEW_ORDER: iso, threequarter, topdown, brawler, side`: the order game.nextView() cycles through when a game doesn't set game.views ('overhead' is left out)\n223:- `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale = 1, zBoost = 1) → view`: new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost): an orthographic camera (degrees; scale = screen pixels per world unit; zBoost stretches heights)\n244:- `E.charView(view)`: the view a rig in `view` is drawn from (the sprite-like angle above; the view itself outside pitch 40-89)\n486:- `game.nextView(dir = 1)`: cycle through game.views (or every view): bind it to a key for players who like to switch\n488:- `game.onView(f)` †: call f(view) whenever the view changes (re-bake caches, move HUD)\n494:- `game.rotateView(deg)` †: turn the camera around the vertical axis in ground views: game.rotateView(45) (degrees). Side views never turn\n497:- `game.setView(v)`: switch the view: an id from E.VIEWS ('iso', 'side', ...) or a View; keeps zoom and turn\n1102:- `E.shapeSprite(id, o = {})`: a shape as a sprite (E.sprite's kind: r.sprite, px.sprite, inventories, HUDs, portraits): E.shapeSprite(id, { view: 'iso', facing, size (its scale, 1), mats, params, outline: true | a color | false }). The view is a name or a View, as given (r.shape draws steep views from the characters' angle: E.charView(view) gives that); a bag icon is the item's shape seen at an angle. r.sprite stands it on its feet (foot: [x, y] in the sprite) unless an anchor is given","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c23f3159-3d46-4aa8-a3bf-9445ed47c313","uuid":"06816654-2653-4fe1-905a-b0cd44954bcb","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:51:20.681Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1:# my-3D2dge guide for AI models\n7:## North star\n17:## The mental model\n29:## Remake workflow (port, remaster, reimagine, remix)\n44:## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\n64:## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\n72:## Hard rules\n82:## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\n120:## Frame order\n140:## Systems in more depth\n142:### Views and resolution\n150:### Scenes\n155:### Camera\n163:### Players (two to four on one machine)\n169:### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\n178:### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n190:### Heights: roofs, ledges, sight and reach\n222:### PlatformMap (side-scrolling levels)\n242:### Platformer (side-scroller controller)\n249:### Body (ground-plane physics)\n255:### Mover and actions (third-person ground controller)\n267:### Perception (sight, hearing, suspicion)\n273:### Attack (melee timing)\n280:### Bullets and patterns\n286:### Humanoid\n297:### Audio\n305:### Text and UI\n311:### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\n319:### Lighting\n328:### Errors and warnings\n338:### Testing without a browser\n357:### The handoff package\n360:### Modules (general systems in files of their own)\n367:### Kits and the diet (which file for which game)\n374:### Recorded sessions (a bug from a phone, replayed exactly)\n402:## Genre recipes\n606:## Building a big game: lessons from Emberdeep\n631:## Where to find more: animation and examples\n648:## Performance\n656:## Checklist before handing a game back\n----\n55:- 219 Views (projections); E.View: view\n223:- `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale = 1, zBoost = 1) → view`: new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost): an orthographic camera (degrees; scale = screen pixels per world unit; zBoost stretches heights)\n221:- `E.VIEWS: iso, threequarter, topdown, brawler, side, overhead`: the ready-made views: iso (yaw 45, pitch 30), threequarter (pitch 55), topdown (80), brawler (25), side (0) and overhead (90, scale 1: world x/y = screen pixels); new E.Game({ view: 'iso' }) and game.setView('side') take their names\n222:- `E.VIEW_ORDER: iso, threequarter, topdown, brawler, side`: the order game.nextView() cycles through when a game doesn't set game.views ('overhead' is left out)\n223:- `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale = 1, zBoost = 1) → view`: new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost): an orthographic camera (degrees; scale = screen pixels per world unit; zBoost stretches heights)\n244:- `E.charView(view)`: the view a rig in `view` is drawn from (the sprite-like angle above; the view itself outside pitch 40-89)\n486:- `game.nextView(dir = 1)`: cycle through game.views (or every view): bind it to a key for players who like to switch\n488:- `game.onView(f)` †: call f(view) whenever the view changes (re-bake caches, move HUD)\n494:- `game.rotateView(deg)` †: turn the camera around the vertical axis in ground views: game.rotateView(45) (degrees). Side views never turn\n497:- `game.setView(v)`: switch the view: an id from E.VIEWS ('iso', 'side', ...) or a View; keeps zoom and turn\n1102:- `E.shapeSprite(id, o = {})`: a shape as a sprite (E.sprite's kind: r.sprite, px.sprite, inventories, HUDs, portraits): E.shapeSprite(id, { view: 'iso', facing, size (its scale, 1), mats, params, outline: true | a color | false }). The view is a name or a View, as given (r.shape draws steep views from the characters' angle: E.charView(view) gives that); a bag icon is the item's shape seen at an angle. r.sprite stands it on its feet (foot: [x, y] in the sprite) unless an anchor is given","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0131WumrgmpzrQ8mjiwZ8Wdc","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
