{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi4q14QfUxM7b888Dehv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The hook's condition is met as far as the TASK.md features go: the bundle exists, every feature has a working mechanic, the exit interview parses and answers every field, and the four sections are below. The earlier \"still not in the game\" list is closed. I haven't checked every new mechanic by playing it, though, and those gaps are listed below.\n\n**What was added this round**\n- **Six teams:** the Magic Circle XI (the Conjurers) and Thrumpton's Thunderbolts, each with an eleven.\n- **The Thunderbolts' ghosts** phase in and out for half a second in every six, so the ball passes through them.\n- **Five grounds:** Dustbowl Downs has rough outside off that turns the spinners, and dust devils deflect aerial balls. The Megadome is the fifth ground.\n- **Five chapters**, with the prologue and ending. The fourth is rained off in its second innings; the Kerfuffle Method revises the target.\n- **Ten showreel segments**, with the perfect-timing cheat on alternate segments so wickets fall.\n- **Eleven Nets lessons**, each with a goal that a match must meet, and a pass/try-again screen.\n- **Co-op and 2-v-2.** The setup has a \"1 v 1 / 2 v 2 / CO-OP\" row, and each side's human players are routed to the batter, the non-striker's run calls, the bowler, and the fielder.\n- **Stumping:** a charged batter who misses can be stumped. Lightning Stumping triples the chance for one over.\n- **Specials:** 21 in total, including Triple Threat (two holograms beside the real ball), the Grudge Drive, and Lightning Stumping.\n- **Instant replays and highlights:** a six, a wicket, a bonus or a special records a clip, and the results screen can play the best three.\n- **Third-umpire screen:** a run-out decided by under 0.15 s shows the call, then the verdict.\n- **High-score table:** a top-five Showdown score asks for initials.\n- **Owl-Eye review, Thunderbolts ghosts, stumping, specials, left-handers, charge and dive** are all in the match.\n- **Replay setting:** it works again, since there are replays to control.\n\n**Verification**\n- `node test/run.mjs` runs 219 checks across eight suites: physics, simulation, specials, features, game flow, the AI Hub mock, the bundle, and browser checks. The browser checks include the AI Hub harness for 1, 2 and 4 players.\n- The features suite runs each mechanic: stumping, the story rain, ghost phasing, the third-umpire call, the layouts, the replay clip, the table's qualifying rule, and the attract showreel's ten segments.\n- No TODO, FIXME, placeholder or stub text is in the source.\n\n**Not fully verified**\n- Lessons 1, 2 and 4 are passed by a bot in tests. Lessons 3, 5 to 11 depend on a person's inputs, so the tests only check that they start. Lesson 8 begins with a full meter.\n- The third-umpire screen and the Owl-Eye panel are drawn from the match's own record, but I haven't screenshotted them.\n- Co-op and 2-v-2 are checked for routing (how many players each side gets), not for a full played match.\n- Triple Threat's holograms are visual; only the real ball is simulated, as PLAN.md specifies.\n- Instant replays play the recorded positions as dots, not the full rigs.\n- The title \"Cricket Kerfuffle\" is the one PLAN.md recorded from its own RNG pick. I did not redo the 50-title brainstorm, because a new title would rename every deliverable.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 06:48:07\n- Source: Cricket (over the top arcade style)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre and perspective: arcade sports (cricket) with a comedy story; broadcast camera behind the bowler, with a wide follow camera for lofted balls\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (no engine file edited), AI Hub client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript, Web Audio; tests on Node 24, Playwright 1.63.0, and Chromium 153\n- Art direction: the engine's HD pixel Humanoids; a TileMap ground with mown stripes, creases and rope; cricket kits, bats, stumps, a sightscreen, a peacock and a cow drawn in code\n- Animation: the engine's rigs posed every step; the swing, the bowler's release, the umpire's signals and the catch celebration use engine moves\n- Audio: chip-synth effects and three songs; Animalese-style formant voices for the commentary and players; a noise crowd; all in code, no audio files\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, parallel Super Over for 1 to 4 players\n- Testing: `node test/run.mjs` (eight suites, 219 checks)\n- Research: none beyond the engine docs and the hub protocol\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux under WSL2; Chromium 153 for the browser checks\n- Model: claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: cost unknown; about 0.8M tokens (estimate from the budget counter); about 1.5 hours\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues found: about a dozen in the first round and about ten in this one, all fixed. Among them: the rigs never animated, the bat and pitch didn't draw, the hub round played two innings, the idle timer ran late, the touch gate was off, the input preset dropped the menu actions, and several settings and dev toggles did nothing.\n- Critical (game-breaking for their feature): the hub round, the rigs and bat, and the idle timer. All three are fixed, and none is known to be open.\n- Most helpful: the headless match runs to a result, with the 15-second in-play cap and the scorecard check. The features suite checks each mechanic by running it.\n- Least helpful: the toss timing check, which had to be rewritten after the flow changed. Lessons 3 and 5 to 11 can only be fully checked by a person playing them.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the View system and the rig draw, with `r.w` projection for every point.\n- Stuck or guessed wrong: a custom input preset without the defaults warns; actor drawing uses the figure's local space, so absolute coordinates draw nothing. Each cost time once.\n- Built ourselves: the ball physics and predictor, timing, fielders, running, the umpire, the bat, the specials, the replays and the Owl-Eye.\n- Hardest: drawing inside actors, and the font's missing symbols (×, σ, °, ²), which showed as question marks.\n- Changed in the engine: none. Every change is in the game's own source.\n- One thing to change: a two-handed bat-grip rig option, and a way to draw world points inside an actor's callback.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the exact exit-interview shape, and the explicit hub and testing rules.\n- Confusing: PLAN.md and TASK.md disagree on the folder name, file name and model suffix. TASK.md won, but reconciling them cost time.\n- Hardest: scope. The first pass cut five grounds, six teams, five chapters, around twenty specials, ten showreel segments and the Nets lessons to fit one session. The second pass restored them. The brief's \"feature-complete\" is what made the cuts unacceptable.\n- Change: a must-have list, a scope budget, and one set of names across the task and the plan."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1510,"cache_read_input_tokens":754709,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1510},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c23f3159-3d46-4aa8-a3bf-9445ed47c313","uuid":"7d3599af-95c8-4fde-b1c4-f554e13fbae6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:44:37.903Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi4pzfqtdxtCkMVdVXkz"}
