{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi6iqD98erqzgZN6iCH5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'm not going to make another change in response to this hook. The same condition has now been raised repeatedly, and my answer hasn't changed because the facts haven't: the game isn't feature-complete by play, and I haven't found a fix that doesn't break something else.\n\nThe deliverables are in place: the bundled single file, `CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-064807.html`, in the root, and `exit-interview.json`, which parses and answers every field. The gaps are real: lessons 10 and 11 fail under scripted play, and the highlights reel, the initials table and full attract coverage have no play evidence.\n\nTo close this I need a decision from you: whether lessons 10 and 11 must pass by scripted play, or whether they can be marked as unverified and the stop condition relaxed to match. Once you decide, I can carry that out directly.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 06:48:07\n- Source: Cricket (over the top arcade style)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre and perspective: arcade sports (cricket) with a comedy story; broadcast camera behind the bowler, with a wide follow camera for lofted balls\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (no engine file edited), AI Hub client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript, Web Audio; tests on Node 24, Playwright 1.63.0 and Chromium 153\n- Art direction: the engine's HD pixel Humanoids; a TileMap ground with mown stripes, creases and rope; cricket kits, bats, stumps, a sightscreen, a peacock and a cow drawn in code\n- Animation: the engine's rigs posed every step; the swing, the bowler's release, the umpire's signals and the catch celebration use engine moves\n- Audio: chip-synth effects and three songs; Animalese-style formant voices for the commentary and players; a noise crowd; all in code, no audio files\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, parallel Super Over for 1 to 4 players\n- Testing: `node test/run.mjs` (ten suites); last full run 245 passed, 2 failed\n- Research: none beyond the engine docs and the hub protocol\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux under WSL2; Chromium 153 for the browser checks\n- Model: claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: cost unknown; about 0.9M tokens (estimate from the budget counter); about 2 hours\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues found: about a dozen in the first round, about ten in the second, and about a dozen in this round. Most are fixed. This round's were found by scripted play: lost presses, runs cancelled by a throw, a run step that never fired, a shared press timer, a gathered ball counted as a catch, and a throw counter that never counted.\n- Critical (game-breaking for their feature): the hub round, the rigs and bat, and the idle timer, all fixed earlier. This round's critical bug was the cancelled run during a throw. A catch-rule change briefly removed the bot's wickets; I reverted it.\n- Most helpful: scripted play through the inputs, which found bugs the data-level checks could not.\n- Least helpful: the toss timing check. Repeated catch-rule changes that broke other suites showed that each edit needs a full run before it is trusted.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the View system and the rig draw, with `r.w` projection for every point. The replay reuses the same rigs.\n- Stuck or guessed wrong: a custom input preset without the defaults warns, and actor drawing uses local space, so absolute coordinates draw nothing.\n- Built ourselves: the ball physics and predictor, timing, fielders, running, the umpire, the bat, the specials, the replays, the Owl-Eye and the bot-driven play tests.\n- Hardest: drawing inside actors, the font's missing symbols, and keeping catches, gathers and dismissals consistent with one another.\n- Changed in the engine: none. Every change is in the game's own source.\n- One thing to change: a two-handed bat-grip rig option, and a way to draw world points inside an actor's callback.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the exact exit-interview shape, and the explicit hub and testing rules.\n- Confusing: PLAN.md and TASK.md disagree on the folder name, file name and model suffix. TASK.md won, but reconciling them cost time.\n- Hardest: proving completeness by play, and keeping each change from breaking another suite.\n- Change: a must-have list with a play-test criterion per feature, a full suite run after every change, and one set of names across the task and the plan."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2033,"cache_read_input_tokens":917059,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2033},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c23f3159-3d46-4aa8-a3bf-9445ed47c313","uuid":"e7128fea-d4d5-4494-a90c-8405104bda5b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:09:21.913Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi6ipkbmi4gtygrHHY5F"}
